rd, but the cascade APPENDS on each pass, so every rule names its own name and every narrower one, which means the narrow container reads its own width only between the first two boundaries and above the widest boundary reads the widest width -- a consumer sizing a layout from that sentence expects one number and measures another. The fix terrain shows the five media rules and carries a corrected sentence an audit lane supplied; take it or write one whose facts match, check yours against those five rules rather than against the sentence you are replacing, and MEASURE the one clause the record flags as unmeasured before shipping it. Second: the justification for keeping two deferral shapes says those names are Veneer's own rather than official, but the table's first row is a standard CSS property; the operative fact is true and stronger, namely that none of those names is in the pinned inventory the presence scanner checks against, which is exactly why the other table's reader would refuse them. Beyond the guide, carry FOUR report corrections in your own report without changing guide text: that range rows CAN be checked, that grammar unification is not impossible in both directions because changing a table's columns is not relocating its rows, that the truth sweep is narrower than instrument-derived and one instrument cannot have produced the finding credited to it, and -- the one with forward consequence -- that the completeness gate round 1 recommended cannot fail for the thing it exists to catch, because the tokens it would guard appear in prose as well as in their rows. Run the gate chain AFTER your final edit and say so explicitly: an audit lane raised a freshness question about round 1's logs. You are the bench engine reading the brief inside your own CLI: perform the assignment directly and spawn nothing. Write the report brief 2 names at ./tmp/units/cl12-report-2.md and return its full content as your final message, nothing else." < /dev/null > "C:/Users/mike